#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# erfc

#% text
# `erfc(x)` gives the complementary error function `1 - erf(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `erfc(x)` is `2/sqrt(pi)` times the integral of `exp(-t^2)` for `t` from `x` to `inf` (DLMF 7.2.2). It decreases from 2 to 0.

#% item
# Unlike `1 - erf(x)`, it stays accurate deep in the right tail, where the value is far below the machine epsilon.

#% item
# `erfc(0)` is 1; other exact arguments stay unevaluated. Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% item
# `erfc` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
erfc(1.0)
#% output
#= 0.15729920705028513

#% cell
erfc(0)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
erfc(-1.0)
#% output
#= 1.8427007929497148

#% text
# 30 digits:

#% cell
n(erfc(2), 30)
#% output
#= 0.004677734981047265837930743632747071

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Deep in the tail:

#% cell
erfc(10.0)
#% output
#= 2.088487583762545e-45

#% cell
erfc(25.0)
#% output
#= 8.300172571196523e-274

#% text
# A complex argument:

#% cell
erfc(1.0 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= -0.3161512816979477 - 0.19045346923783468im

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The probability that a standard normal value exceeds 6:

#% cell
erfc(6/sqrt(2.0))/2
#% output
#= 9.865876450377014e-10

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `erf` and `erfc` add up to 1:

#% cell
erf(0.7) + erfc(0.7)
#% output
#= 1.0

#% text
# The inverse:

#% cell
inverse_erfc(erfc(1.5))
#% output
#= 1.5

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `erf`, `erfi`, `inverse_erfc`, `normal_distribution`
